=========================================================
1. Keywords
=========================================================


Definition:
Keywords are reserved words in JavaScript.
They have a predefined meaning and cannot be used
as variable names, function names, or identifiers.

Examples:
abstract
arguments
await
boolean
break
byte
case
catch


=========================================================
2. Identifiers
=========================================================
Definition:
Identifiers are the names of programming entities like:

- Variables
- Functions
- Objects
- Classes
etc.

=========================================================
3. Rules of Identifiers
=========================================================


i) We cannot use any JavaScript keyword as an identifier.

ii) We cannot start an identifier with a digit,
    but digits can be used after the first character.

    Correct:
    student1
    marks2025

    Wrong:
    1student

iii) We cannot use special characters except
     underscore (_) and dollar sign ($).

     Correct:
     student_name
     $price

     Wrong:
     student-name
     student@123

iv) We cannot use the same identifier name
    more than once in the same scope
    (for let and const declarations).


=========================================================
4. Data Types
=========================================================
Number:
Represents whole numbers and decimal numbers.
Examples:
10
20.36
-25.35
-50
+25.36
+20

>>> String:
    Collection of characters.
Examples:
"Sample"
'Example'
"Hello World"

>>> Boolean:
    Represents only two values.
true
false

>>> Other JavaScript Data Types:
null
undefined
object
bigint
array
set
map


=========================================================
5. Variables
=========================================================

Variables are used when data is not fixed.

Definition:

Variables are names of memory locations in RAM
that store some information.

OR

Variables are placeholders (data containers)
used to store values.


Syntax:

>>> Function Scope
    var variableName = value;

>>> Block Scope
    let variableName2 = value;

>>> Block Scope (Read Only)
    const variableName3 = value;

=========================================================
6. Constant / Literals
=========================================================

Definition:
Constant (Literals) are used when data is fixed.

Types of Literals:
1. Boolean
   true
   false

2. Number
   10
   20
   -25
   15.36
   +25.25
   -65.25

3. String
   "Simple"
   'Simple'
=========================================================
Examples
=========================================================*/

var a = 10;
var b = 10.5;
var c = "Hello World";
var d = 'Hello World';
var e = `Hello World`;

var f = "100";
var g = true;
var h = false;
var i = "true";


// Checking Data Types


console.log(a, typeof a);
console.log(b, typeof b);
console.log(c, typeof c);
console.log(d, typeof d);
console.log(e, typeof e);

console.log(f, typeof f);
console.log(g, typeof g);
console.log(h, typeof h);
console.log(i, typeof i);


/*
=========================================================
7. Tokens
=========================================================

Definition:

Tokens are the smallest individual units
of a JavaScript program.

Types of Tokens:
1. Keywords
2. Identifiers
3. Constants / Literals
4. Operators
5. Punctuators
*/

var a = 10;

/*
Explanation:

var  -> Keyword
a    -> Identifier (Variable)
=    -> Operator
10   -> Constant / Literal
;    -> Punctuator

Other Punctuators:
Space
( )
{ }
[ ]
,
.
;
:

=========================================================
8. Output Instructions
=========================================================

console.log() is used to display output
in the browser console.
*/

var a = 10;
var b = 20;

var sum = a + b;

console.log(sum);

console.log(a, "+", b, "=", sum);

console.log(a + " + " + b + " = " + sum);

// Template Literal (String Template)
console.log(`${a} + ${b} = ${sum}`);

// Multiline Template Literal
console.log(`
 ${a}
+${b}
=${sum}
`);

